import { docsLinks } from '../lib/nav';
---

<DocShell
   title="Recording sessions"
   summary="a11ied can capture real assistive technology sessions as video when the host and target support it. That feature exists to preserve evidence, not to make compliance claims magically bigger."
   links={docsLinks}
>
   <section>
      <h2>Supported scope</h2>
      <ul>
         <li>real VoiceOver sessions on macOS</li>
         <li>real NVDA sessions on Windows</li>
         <li>commands that own or start a real target session themselves</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
         The virtual target is not treated as a recording target for this release line. A headless fast
         path and a real screen recording are different products, so the CLI keeps them separate.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2>Examples</h2>
      <pre><code>node packages/cli/dist/cli.js drive start --target voiceover --recording ./recordings/voiceover-home.mov
node packages/cli/dist/cli.js run pattern dialog_probe --target voiceover --url http://127.0.0.1:6173/dialog.html --recording ./recordings/dialog-probe.mov --json
node packages/cli/dist/cli.js verify criterion 4.1.3 --target voiceover --url http://127.0.0.1:6173/status-message.html --recording ./recordings/status-message.mov --json</code></pre>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2>What doctor is checking</h2>
      <p>
         The macOS doctor flow probes the native recording path that a11ied actually uses. That matters
         because recording failures are often host-app permission problems, not Guidepup bugs.
      </p>
      <pre><code>node packages/cli/dist/cli.js doctor
node packages/cli/dist/cli.js doctor --json</code></pre>
      <p>The installed CLI exposes the same check as <code>a11ied doctor</code>.</p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
      <p>
         Run <code>doctor</code> before blaming Guidepup. The doctor report now probes the native macOS
         recording path that a11ied actually uses and tells you when the current host app cannot record
         its own screen.
      </p>
      <p>
         On macOS, a11ied records video without asking for microphone input. If the same recording
         command works from Terminal but not from your editor or agent app, the usual problem is
         app-specific Screen Recording permission.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2>Privacy and review caveats</h2>
      <ul>
         <li>recordings may capture page content, system chrome, and assistive-technology output</li>
         <li>a recording is evidence of what happened during one run, not proof of general compliance</li>
         <li>the artifact path belongs in the report so later reviewers can find the exact run that was captured</li>
      </ul>
   </section>

   <section class="field-note">
      <h2>Do not use recording as a shortcut</h2>
      <p>
         A video is useful when someone needs to inspect what VoiceOver or NVDA actually did. It does
         not replace the criterion, the applicability call, or the verdict semantics. It just gives the
         run a stronger trail.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
